    },
    {
      "text": "The controlled comparison includes equiatomic CrFeCoNi and equiatomic CrCoNi as reduced-component subsets.",
      "field": "falsification_experiment.design",
      "needs_support": false,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The quinary, quaternary, and ternary alloys can all be prepared as single-phase FCC materials.",
      "field": "material_initial_state",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::rubric_card::000::eb2c90832c"
      ],
      "same_system": "unclear"
    },
    {
      "text": "Identical thermomechanical processing can produce comparable grain sizes across the alloy variants.",
      "field": "material_initial_state",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The quinary alloy has a higher degree of random chemical disorder than its quaternary and ternary subsets.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Greater random chemical disorder in the quinary alloy suppresses the development of chemical short-range order during thermomechanical processing.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Homogeneous chemical disorder produces a more uniform local lattice-strain field.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "A more uniform local lattice-strain field facilitates dislocation glide.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The quaternary and ternary subsets are more prone than the quinary alloy to short-range ordering or local chemical clustering.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Short-range-ordered regions or chemical clusters act as strong obstacles to dislocation motion.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Dislocation pinning by short-range order increases yield strength.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Short-range order or chemical clustering causes localized strain concentrations.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Localized strain concentrations promote premature failure and reduce ductility.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The quinary alloy has a smoother dislocation-glide energy landscape and more homogeneous plastic deformation than the reduced-component alloys.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Under identical test conditions, the quinary alloy will have lower yield strength than the quaternary and ternary alloys.",
      "field": "predicted_outcome",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Under identical test conditions, the quinary alloy will have greater ductility than the quaternary and ternary alloys.",
      "field": "predicted_outcome",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The quaternary and ternary alloys will exhibit earlier strain localization than the quinary alloy.",
      "field": "predicted_outcome",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The proposed mechanism is applicable primarily to single-phase FCC alloys with similar grain sizes tested at room temperature and a standard strain rate.",
      "field": "boundary",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Changes in processing history, temperature, or testing environment may alter short-range-order dynamics and deformation response.",
      "field": "boundary",
